; worldedit.eval — the world can be painted, and taken back (W3).
;
; The mode, the brush, the paint, and the ONE undo history the world now shares
; with the levels — which is the part worth checking, because it is the claim
; that a step spanning tiers undoes as one thing.
;
; A cell is painted, undone, and redone, and the terrain is read back at each
; point. Reading it back is the whole test: an undo that reported success and
; changed nothing would look identical from the console otherwise.
logecho on
reset
worldedit
worldedit on

; --- arm, and refuse what does not exist ------------------------------------
expect-refuse terrainbrush nonsense
terrainbrush moor
terrainbrush

; --- 6,6 is road; paint it moor ---------------------------------------------
world
paint 6 6
world

; --- painting it again changes nothing --------------------------------------
paint 6 6

; --- and the editor's history takes it back ---------------------------------
undo
world
redo
world

; --- the PLAYER'S map is never the editor (code-review C77, C79) ------------
; Editor mode is still on and a terrain still armed. In a dungeon the M map's
; world page is the same view, but it must stay play: no toolbar, the fog on, a
; left click paints nothing (only the world map state closes a stroke, so a
; paint here escaped the undo history), and a right-click on a doorway opens
; nothing (only the world map state routes its dialogs, so one opened here sat
; over the HUD and could not be closed). `worldview click` goes through the
; view's own Update, a press and a release at the cell's centre. 7,6 is road;
; 10,10 is the crypt_gate doorway.
mappage open
mappage world
worldview
worldview click 7 6 left
worldview click 10 10 right
mappage close

; --- the control: the travel screen, same mode, same clicks ------------------
; There the band is up, the fog off, the click paints (its stroke left for the
; state to close) and the doorway opens the settings dialog - so the clicks
; above are clicks that WOULD have done something.
worldmap on
worldview
worldview click 7 6 left
worldview click 10 10 right
worldsettings off
worldmap off
